<h1>HealTrip AI Patient Decision Assistant</h1>
<p><strong>Technical Evaluation Prototype for HealTrip</strong><br>
<em>Full-Stack Engineering &bull; Clinical Triage &bull; Agentic Tool Calling &bull; Anti-Hallucination Architecture</em></p>

<hr style="border: 0; border-top: 1px solid #cbd5e1; margin: 20px 0;">

<h2>1. Executive Summary & Problem Scope</h2>
<p>When patients experience ambiguous or concerning medical symptoms—such as:</p>
<blockquote>
"I have chest pain and I'm not sure whether I should see a cardiologist, go to the ER, or seek a second opinion."
</blockquote>
<p>A naive conversational LLM presents two catastrophic healthcare risks:</p>
<ol>
    <li><strong>Safety Failure:</strong> Under-triaging acute emergencies (e.g., advising an elective consultation or a remote second opinion when the patient is in fact experiencing Acute Coronary Syndrome).</li>
    <li><strong>Hallucination Risk:</strong> Inventing fictitious clinics, non-existent doctor names, arbitrary pricing, or wrong contact information.</li>
</ol>
<p>The <strong>HealTrip AI Patient Decision Assistant</strong> demonstrates an engineering architecture that systematically mitigates these risks using <strong>deterministic clinical pre-screening, native LLM tool calling, grounded database retrieval, and post-generation verification</strong>.</p>

<h2>2. Architecture & Data Flow</h2>
<pre>
[ Patient / Browser (React 19 SPA) ]
                 │
                 │ JSON over HTTPS (Bilingual English / Arabic)
                 ▼
[ Full-Stack Express Server (server.ts / Vercel Serverless api/index.ts) ]
                 │
                 ├──► Step 1: Clinical Triage Pre-Screening
                 │    Rule-based safety evaluator detecting ACS, Stroke FAST, Meningitis
                 │
                 ├──► Step 2: Gemini 3.8 Flash Agent Orchestration
                 │    System prompts + Function/Tool Calling Declarations
                 │    Tool: search_providers(...) & evaluate_triage_urgency(...)
                 │
                 ├──► Step 3: Structured Database Execution
                 │    Queries indexed registry of JCI-accredited Hospitals & Doctors
                 │    (Istanbul, Dubai, Riyadh, Amman, Berlin, London)
                 │
                 ├──► Step 4: Tool Response Synthesis
                 │    Model incorporates tool query results into clinical explanation
                 │
                 ├──► Step 5: Anti-Hallucination & Grounding Check
                 │    Verifies returned doctor/hospital IDs match database entities
                 │
                 ▼
[ Strongly Typed AgentResponse Payload delivered to Frontend ]
</pre>

<h2>3. AI Agent Design & Tool Calling</h2>
<h3>Core Tools Declared to LLM:</h3>
<ul>
    <li><code>search_providers</code>:
        <ul>
            <li><code>specialty</code> (e.g. Cardiology, Orthopedics, Neurology, Emergency Medicine)</li>
            <li><code>location</code> (City or Country, e.g. Istanbul, Dubai, Riyadh, Germany)</li>
            <li><code>emergency_capable</code> (Boolean: requires 24/7 ER facility)</li>
            <li><code>telehealth_second_opinion</code> (Boolean: seeking remote specialist review)</li>
            <li><code>language</code> (e.g. Arabic, English)</li>
            <li><code>max_fee_usd</code> (Budget ceiling in USD)</li>
        </ul>
    </li>
    <li><code>evaluate_triage_urgency</code>:
        <ul>
            <li><code>symptoms</code> (Array of reported patient symptoms)</li>
            <li><code>red_flags_suspected</code> (Boolean: life-threatening emergency flag)</li>
            <li><code>provisional_condition</code> (Differential diagnosis, e.g. Acute Coronary Syndrome)</li>
        </ul>
    </li>
</ul>

<h3>Multi-Turn Tool Calling Execution:</h3>
<ol>
    <li>The patient submits symptoms in natural language (in English or Arabic).</li>
    <li>The backend initializes the conversation with Gemini 3.8 Flash configured with tool schemas.</li>
    <li>The model inspects clinical presentation and calls <code>search_providers</code> with structured parameters.</li>
    <li>The backend executes the query against verified provider records and returns the function response to the model.</li>
    <li>The model synthesizes a cohesive, empathetic clinical explanation, 2–3 targeted clarifying questions, and verified doctor recommendations.</li>
</ol>

<h2>4. Preventing AI Hallucinations</h2>
<p>Medical applications demand deterministic verification:</p>
<ol>
    <li><strong>Negative Constraint Prompting:</strong> The agent system prompt explicitly prohibits generating fictional clinics, doctors, or phone numbers.</li>
    <li><strong>Server-Side Data Binding:</strong> The provider cards shown in the UI are bound directly to the database query response, preventing synthetic text hallucination.</li>
    <li><strong>Emergency Safety Override:</strong> If acute red flags are detected, the system immediately surfaces local emergency dispatch hotlines (911 / 998 / 112).</li>
    <li><strong>Auditability:</strong> Every tool execution (arguments, matched record count, latency in milliseconds) is recorded and viewable in the Telemetry inspector.</li>
</ol>

<h2>5. Relational Schema Design (PostgreSQL DDL)</h2>
<pre>
CREATE TABLE hospitals (
    id VARCHAR(64) PRIMARY KEY,
    name VARCHAR(255) NOT NULL,
    name_ar VARCHAR(255) NOT NULL,
    city VARCHAR(128) NOT NULL,
    country VARCHAR(128) NOT NULL,
    accreditations TEXT[] NOT NULL,
    emergency_department_247 BOOLEAN DEFAULT FALSE,
    helipad BOOLEAN DEFAULT FALSE,
    languages_supported TEXT[] NOT NULL,
    international_patient_desk BOOLEAN DEFAULT TRUE,
    rating NUMERIC(3, 2) DEFAULT 5.00,
    review_count INTEGER DEFAULT 0,
    telehealth_ready BOOLEAN DEFAULT TRUE,
    address TEXT NOT NULL,
    contact_phone VARCHAR(64) NOT NULL,
    emergency_hotline VARCHAR(64) NOT NULL,
    specialties TEXT[] NOT NULL
);

CREATE INDEX idx_hospitals_city ON hospitals(city);
CREATE INDEX idx_hospitals_emergency ON hospitals(emergency_department_247);

CREATE TABLE doctors (
    id VARCHAR(64) PRIMARY KEY,
    name VARCHAR(255) NOT NULL,
    name_ar VARCHAR(255) NOT NULL,
    title VARCHAR(255) NOT NULL,
    hospital_id VARCHAR(64) REFERENCES hospitals(id) ON DELETE CASCADE,
    specialty VARCHAR(128) NOT NULL,
    subspecialties TEXT[] NOT NULL,
    experience_years INTEGER NOT NULL,
    languages TEXT[] NOT NULL,
    second_opinion_available BOOLEAN DEFAULT TRUE,
    consultation_fee_usd NUMERIC(10, 2) NOT NULL,
    telehealth_fee_usd NUMERIC(10, 2) NOT NULL,
    next_available_slot VARCHAR(128),
    bio TEXT NOT NULL,
    rating NUMERIC(3, 2) DEFAULT 5.00,
    procedures TEXT[] NOT NULL,
    city VARCHAR(128) NOT NULL,
    country VARCHAR(128) NOT NULL
);

CREATE INDEX idx_doctors_specialty ON doctors(specialty);
CREATE INDEX idx_doctors_second_opinion ON doctors(second_opinion_available);
</pre>

<h2>6. Security, Privacy & Reliability</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Zero Secret Exposure:</strong> The Gemini API key is isolated on the server side via <code>process.env.GEMINI_API_KEY</code>. No credentials are ever sent to client browsers.</li>
    <li><strong>Graceful Deterministic Fallback:</strong> If the AI API experiences network disruption or rate limits, the system seamlessly routes through the clinical rule engine, guaranteeing 100% test reliability.</li>
    <li><strong>Bilingual RTL/LTR:</strong> Full dynamic localization supporting English (LTR) and Arabic (العربية, RTL) with proper typography and alignment.</li>
</ul>

<h2>7. How to Run Locally</h2>
<pre>
# 1. Install dependencies (with legacy peer deps handling)
npm install

# 2. Add your Gemini API key in .env (optional; fallback engine operates automatically if absent)
echo 'GEMINI_API_KEY="your-api-key"' > .env

# 3. Start development server (Port 3000)
npm run dev

# 4. Open in your browser:
http://localhost:3000
</pre>

